# 1 Insertion

Section 1 of Lesson 80 already used δ/δJ at J=0. At finite J the same derivative inserts a field into a correlator in the presence of the source, which is how one studies an atom in an external potential without leaving the path integral. In QED, δ/δJ_μ(x) inserts A^μ(x); a Grassmann derivative inserts ψ or ψ̄.

### Worked example 1

How does one insert an extra photon into Compton scattering in this language? Differentiate the four-point electron–photon correlator with respect to an additional J_μ and then amputate, Lesson 51. That is the path-integral form of attaching an external photon line.

# 2 Shifts of the dummy variable

The name of φ is dummy: ∫Dφ F[φ]=∫Dφ F[φ+ε] whenever Dφ is invariant under φ→φ+ε. Expanding to first order in ε(x) gives

$$ ⟨ δS[φ] / δφ(x) + J(x) ⟩_J = 0

which is the Schwinger–Dyson equation, the path-integral Euler–Lagrange equation in the presence of J. Completing the square in Lesson 80 was the special case of a linear shift that cancels J against Kφ.

The shift is illegal if the measure is not invariant (a Jacobian), or if the contour at infinity does not allow it. Faddeev–Popov (Lesson 70, Lesson 79) is the Jacobian for a gauge shift.

# 3 Ward identities from a symmetry

If S is invariant under a shift, δS=0, and if Dφ is invariant too, then ⟨δ(operators)⟩=0. For a local U(1) shift of A_μ and of the electron phase, this is Lesson 71: k_μ ℳ^μ=0, now read as a statement about Z rather than about operator gauge transformations.

### Worked example 2

Why did Lesson 71 need both Compton diagrams? In the path integral, a gauge shift of A_μ acts on every occurrence of A in S_int. Every external photon, attached wherever the electron line allows, must be included or δS would not vanish inside the correlator. Dropping a diagram is dropping a term in δS.

# 4 What this does not add

These operations reorganize Parts V–X. They do not compute a new cross section and they do not remove ultraviolet divergences. They are the language in which Lesson 83 will re-derive the Feynman rules.

